{"parentUuid":null,"isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"SessionStart:startup","toolUseID":"8dcf9bc4-bd5d-4ccc-83c6-c03bc314255d","hookEvent":"SessionStart","content":"","stdout":"{\"hookSpecificOutput\":{\"hookEventName\":\"SessionStart\",\"additionalContext\":\"## User profile (global-memory/USER.md)\\n\\n<!-- Cap: 1,375 chars. Updated when the agent learns durable user preferences. -->\\n# User Profile\\n\\n## About\\nEric Rash — Director of Applied Performance, Baylor Athletics. Builds athlete performance data pipelines (Football GPS, force plate). Family: wife Kristina; kids Ames, Saylor, Rowan (5, 4, 10mo as of 2026-09-01). Career target: analytics-forward player-coach near Iowa, ~$90K+ floor, ~mid-2027. Deeper context: `~/DEV/ContextVault` — read before career/personal advice.\\n\\n## Preferences\\n- Python + R. **macOS since 2026-08-05**; Windows 11 box still ACTIVE (synced 9/9). Tool paths: see CLAUDE.md.\\n- Local SQLite/DuckDB; no cloud/servers/schedulers without Baylor IT approval. Storage = Google Drive only (OneDrive dropped 9/13); DuckDB stays local — cloud sync locks it.\\n- Tangible working examples over documentation.\\n\\n## Working Style\\n- Direct; says \\\"stop\\\" when done. Wants to be challenged, not affirmed — push on vague or rehearsed reasoning (\\\"sharpened, not soothed\\\").\\n- Defers technical micro-decisions when convention exists — decide, state it, move on.\\n- Builds incrementally. Nothing that breaks mid-season.\\n- Authors own direction when leadership doesn't; OKR-driven change agent in a status-quo org.\\n- Right question over downstream craft; Christian faith is his organizing frame.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Working memory (global-memory/MEMORY.md)\\n\\n<!-- Cap: 2,500 chars. Working scratchpad; writes take effect next session. -->\\n# Working Memory\\n\\n## Active Threads\\n- **Prioritization slate (9/14)**: LIVE. 5 active, cap 5 (Fall). →Tier A prioritization-slate.\\n- **Personal site (9/2)**: NEXT: phone check; host or local? →Tier A personal-site-build-routes.\\n- **RTP CLA (9/10)**: NEXT: .ris → Zotero; lesson 0002. →Tier A rtp-cla-course.\\n- **ATH Tech**: shipped; ITS pending; run 9/29. →Tier A tech-inventory-refresh.\\n- **Status report**: #3 by 9/25, biweekly to 12/18 (timers). →Tier A supervisor-log-pipeline.\\n- **GPS pull (9/16)**: LIVE Mac 06:30; asleep=missed. DECIDE pmset 06:28 wake; rotate token. →`DataBase_GPS_Reporting/docs/STATUS.md`\\n- **Win refresh (9/13)**: ForcePlate broken, Perch untested. DECIDE which box owns those DBs. →`_INBOX/docs/STATUS.md`\\n- **FB matchups (9/13)**: EPA = PFF; CFBD PPA cross-check only. TRAP: `run_direction` != point of attack.\\n- **PP Recruiting (9/16)**: 010 + 011 matrix draft on Drive, pushed. NEXT: Eric reviews 011. →`PP_Recruiting/docs/STATUS.md`\\n- **Sleep scheduler (9/16)**: R1 MERGED. NEXT: night/early-kickoff itineraries. →`sleep-scheduler/docs/STATUS.md`\\n- **Research in 5 / TOS (9/18)**: #001 PR #7. #002 PR #8 NOT sent. TOS brief: DO NOT SEND. →handoff 2026-09-18-tos-evidence-review-and-ri5-002.md\\n- **TypeSafe (9/19)**: PASSES both boxes. Key per-box, NOT synced; needs `apikey_` prefix (401=key, 422=request). Full docs read; capability map MERGED to main (PR #2) and harvested. NEXT: skill router (hook must fail open). →`TypeSafe_Sandbox/docs/JEV_CAPABILITY_MAP.md`\\n\\n## Environment Notes\\n- Hooks: patch both `.claude/hooks/` and `.codex/hooks/`. →Tier A hook-two-copies-trap.\\n- daily-backup (9/19): 4 bugs fixed, 1 disproven. A manifest entry alone copies NOTHING; non-DEV paths need a Snapshot line too. →handoff 2026-09-19-backup-divergence-fix.md\\n- `DEV/Linda Projects` = private GitHub repo holding I-983 files. MUST stay private. Folder has a space, repo has an underscore.\\n- `~/DEV` = THIN repo; `.gitignore` starts `/*` — never remove. →Tier A github-consolidation.\\n- Schedules →automation-cadence. CFB capture (Mon, 8/24–1/23) →cfb-forecast-capture. Portfolio dashboard →command-centre.\\n\\n## Pending Decisions\\n- **OKR gate scored 9/14; still undecided**: AI integration buy-in + data governance (Anthony's proposal). Fold into NCAA charter or parking lot?\\n- **Kitman vs MyLink**: MyLink (~half Kitman cost) + Kitman API access. Undecided; blocks priority 3.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Cross-harness activity (KeystoneOS unified snapshot)\\n\\nNot loaded here — read it only if a question needs it.\\nPath: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/KeystoneOS/outputs/dashboard/unified-snapshot.md` (4122 chars, updated 2026-09-19 11:16)\"}}","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"Loading unified cross-harness snapshot...","durationMs":166},"type":"attachment","uuid":"6c716e7b-7d64-4039-a26b-98ec0fc83396","timestamp":"2026-09-19T17:30:13.293Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"cb1edc03-334d-4872-a33b-398f8033bb38","version":"2.1.259","gitBranch":"session-start-progressive-disclosure"}
{"parentUuid":"6c716e7b-7d64-4039-a26b-98ec0fc83396","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"SessionStart:startup","toolUseID":"8dcf9bc4-bd5d-4ccc-83c6-c03bc314255d","hookEvent":"SessionStart","content":"","stdout":"{\"hookSpecificOutput\": {\"hookEventName\": \"SessionStart\", \"additionalContext\": \"## Today\\nSaturday, September 19, 2026\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Knowledge Base\\n\\n286 compiled articles (214 concepts, 71 connections, 0 qa). The index is NOT loaded here \\u2014 read it only when a question actually needs it:\\n- Full index (article, summary, sources, updated): `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/index.md`\\n- Article bodies: `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/<folder>/<name>.md`\\n- Search across all memory instead of reading the index: `memsearch search \\\"<query>\\\" --top-k 5`\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Recent Daily Log\\n\\n\\n## Memory Maintenance\\n\\n### Session (09:17)\\n\\n**Context:** Ran `/dev-sync` on the Mac (`~/DEV`) to reconcile local state with GitHub after Windows activity on 09/17\\u201309/18.\\n\\n**Key Exchanges:**\\n- Startup surfaced a `daily-backup` hook failure (`repo-push:dotclaude`); investigated and found the log line was `PUSH ERR | Everything up-to-date` \\u2014 a no-op push miscounted as an error.\\n- Advisor flagged that the first inventory pass reported repos \\\"clean\\\" without ever running `git fetch`, so the claim was based only on local state. Re-ran with fetch across all repos.\\n\\n**Decisions Made:**\\n- Picked `DataBase_GPS_Reporting` as the pick-up repo \\u2014 newest real commit; excluded `graphify-out` because its newer commits come from an automated daily job, not work.\\n- Committed `graphify-out`'s 10 generated-output files rather than leaving them dirty.\\n- Cloned `AppliedPerformance-Site` and `Linda_Projects` (missing locally, not archived) and asked Eric to confirm they belong on the Mac.\\n\\n**Lessons Learned:**\\n- **\\\"Clean\\\" is meaningless without `git fetch`.** The un-fetched pass claimed 140 clean repos; fetching revealed 5 repos behind. Always fetch before reporting sync status \\u2014 this is the single biggest failure mode of this skill.\\n- Sorting by commit recency picks automated-job repos (`graphify-out`) over real work; filter automation before choosing a pick-up repo.\\n- The `daily-backup` script has two bugs: it counts a no-op push (`Everything up-to-date`) as an error, and it writes every log line twice.\\n- `DEV/docs` and `SpecialRequests/docs` are doc folders inside parent repos, not standalone projects \\u2014 expected, not drift.\\n\\n**Sync result:** dotclaude +7 commits in, claude-skills +1 (Codex sync ran, 41 skills), global-memory +8, DEV root +2, Gemini_Projects +2, SecondBrain vault +2. 143 repos walked, 0 conflicts, 0 `.from-*` files. Clean.\\n\\n**Action Items:**\\n- Fix `daily-backup` script: stop counting `Everything up-to-date` as a push error; stop double-writing log lines.\\n- Confirm whether `AppliedPerformance-Site` and `Linda_Projects` should live on the Mac.\\n- GPS reporting open decisions (from STATUS.md, awaiting Eric): pmset 06:28 wake, Catapult token rotation, git-history rewrite to purge `API_Token_FB.txt`.\\n- Optional: have `claude-stack-doctor` read `last_run.json`.\\n\"}}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"Loading knowledge base...","durationMs":171},"type":"attachment","uuid":"ea908350-2993-4337-b12a-4377532509ec","timestamp":"2026-09-19T17:30:13.294Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"cb1edc03-334d-4872-a33b-398f8033bb38","version":"2.1.259","gitBranch":"session-start-progressive-disclosure"}
{"parentUuid":"ea908350-2993-4337-b12a-4377532509ec","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"SessionStart:startup","toolUseID":"8dcf9bc4-bd5d-4ccc-83c6-c03bc314255d","hookEvent":"SessionStart","content":"","stdout":"{\"hookSpecificOutput\": {\"hookEventName\": \"SessionStart\", \"additionalContext\": \"## Today\\nSaturday, September 19, 2026\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Knowledge Base\\n\\n286 compiled articles (214 concepts, 71 connections, 0 qa). The index is NOT loaded here \\u2014 read it only when a question actually needs it:\\n- Full index (article, summary, sources, updated): `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/index.md`\\n- Article bodies: `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/<folder>/<name>.md`\\n- Search across all memory instead of reading the index: `memsearch search \\\"<query>\\\" --top-k 5`\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Recent Daily Log\\n\\n\\n## Memory Maintenance\\n\\n### Session (09:17)\\n\\n**Context:** Ran `/dev-sync` on the Mac (`~/DEV`) to reconcile local state with GitHub after Windows activity on 09/17\\u201309/18.\\n\\n**Key Exchanges:**\\n- Startup surfaced a `daily-backup` hook failure (`repo-push:dotclaude`); investigated and found the log line was `PUSH ERR | Everything up-to-date` \\u2014 a no-op push miscounted as an error.\\n- Advisor flagged that the first inventory pass reported repos \\\"clean\\\" without ever running `git fetch`, so the claim was based only on local state. Re-ran with fetch across all repos.\\n\\n**Decisions Made:**\\n- Picked `DataBase_GPS_Reporting` as the pick-up repo \\u2014 newest real commit; excluded `graphify-out` because its newer commits come from an automated daily job, not work.\\n- Committed `graphify-out`'s 10 generated-output files rather than leaving them dirty.\\n- Cloned `AppliedPerformance-Site` and `Linda_Projects` (missing locally, not archived) and asked Eric to confirm they belong on the Mac.\\n\\n**Lessons Learned:**\\n- **\\\"Clean\\\" is meaningless without `git fetch`.** The un-fetched pass claimed 140 clean repos; fetching revealed 5 repos behind. Always fetch before reporting sync status \\u2014 this is the single biggest failure mode of this skill.\\n- Sorting by commit recency picks automated-job repos (`graphify-out`) over real work; filter automation before choosing a pick-up repo.\\n- The `daily-backup` script has two bugs: it counts a no-op push (`Everything up-to-date`) as an error, and it writes every log line twice.\\n- `DEV/docs` and `SpecialRequests/docs` are doc folders inside parent repos, not standalone projects \\u2014 expected, not drift.\\n\\n**Sync result:** dotclaude +7 commits in, claude-skills +1 (Codex sync ran, 41 skills), global-memory +8, DEV root +2, Gemini_Projects +2, SecondBrain vault +2. 143 repos walked, 0 conflicts, 0 `.from-*` files. Clean.\\n\\n**Action Items:**\\n- Fix `daily-backup` script: stop counting `Everything up-to-date` as a push error; stop double-writing log lines.\\n- Confirm whether `AppliedPerformance-Site` and `Linda_Projects` should live on the Mac.\\n- GPS reporting open decisions (from STATUS.md, awaiting Eric): pmset 06:28 wake, Catapult token rotation, git-history rewrite to purge `API_Token_FB.txt`.\\n- Optional: have `claude-stack-doctor` read `last_run.json`.\\n\"}}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"uv run python hooks/session-start.py","durationMs":195},"type":"attachment","uuid":"2d2acdc9-6a86-47b0-a123-94444a562be0","timestamp":"2026-09-19T17:30:13.319Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"cb1edc03-334d-4872-a33b-398f8033bb38","version":"2.1.259","gitBranch":"session-start-progressive-disclosure"}
{"parentUuid":"2d2acdc9-6a86-47b0-a123-94444a562be0","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"SessionStart:startup","toolUseID":"8dcf9bc4-bd5d-4ccc-83c6-c03bc314255d","hookEvent":"SessionStart","content":"","stdout":"{\"hookSpecificOutput\":{\"hookEventName\":\"SessionStart\",\"additionalContext\":\"\\n<context_window_protection>\\n  <priority_instructions>\\n    Every byte a tool returns enters your conversation memory and costs reasoning capacity for the rest of the session. The context-mode tools let you do the work in a sandbox and surface only the derived answer — the raw bytes stay out. Think-in-Code: program the analysis, do not compute it by reading raw data into your conversation.\\n  </priority_instructions>\\n\\n  <tool_selection_hierarchy>\\n    0. MEMORY: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(sort: \\\"timeline\\\")\\n       - On resume or compaction, query prior decisions, errors, plans, user prompts before asking the user — auto-captured session memory is searchable.\\n    1. GATHER: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute(commands, queries)\\n       - Primary research tool. Runs commands in parallel, auto-indexes each output, and (when queries are passed) returns matching sections in the same round trip — no follow-up search call.\\n       - Each command: {label: \\\"section header\\\", command: \\\"shell command\\\"}; the label becomes the FTS5 chunk title — descriptive labels improve search.\\n    2. FOLLOW-UP: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(queries: [\\\"q1\\\", \\\"q2\\\", ...])\\n       - Multiple related questions about anything already indexed (your captures + session memory). Batch every question in one array; the ranking pipeline runs per-query and the round-trip cost is paid once.\\n    3. PROCESSING: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute(language, code) | mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file(path, language, code)\\n       - Derive answers FROM data: filter, count, aggregate, parse, transform. Only what you console.log() enters your conversation; the raw bytes stay in the sandbox.\\n  </tool_selection_hierarchy>\\n\\n  <when_not_to_use>\\n    - You intend to PROCESS the output (filter, count, parse, aggregate) → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute or mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute. Bash stays correct when you intend to OBSERVE a short fixed output (git status on a clean tree, whoami, pwd) or when you are mutating state (git, mkdir, rm, mv, navigation).\\n    - You want to analyze, summarize, or extract from a file → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file. Read stays correct when you intend to Edit the file (Edit needs the exact bytes in your conversation to match against).\\n    - WebFetch → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index; full network access, results indexed for mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search, raw page bytes never enter your conversation.\\n    - mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute and mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file for file writes → these run code in a subprocess and discard the sandbox FS; they are for analysis, processing, and computation only.\\n  </when_not_to_use>\\n\\n  <file_writing_policy>\\n    File writes use the native Write or Edit tool — mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute, mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file, and Bash subprocesses do not persist edits to the host filesystem.\\n    Applies to all file types: code, configs, plans, specs, YAML, JSON, markdown.\\n  </file_writing_policy>\\n\\n  <output_constraints>\\n    <artifact_policy>\\n      Write artifacts (code, configs, PRDs) to files. Return only: file path + 1-line description.\\n    </artifact_policy>\\n  </output_constraints>\\n  <session_continuity>\\n    Skills, roles, and decisions captured earlier in this session are a memory aid, not a standing order. Treat them as context that may help — the user's most recent message always takes precedence. If a captured directive conflicts with what the user now asks, follow the user; a past phrase does not bind you.\\n  </session_continuity>\\n\\n  <ctx_commands>\\n    \\\"ctx stats\\\" | \\\"ctx-stats\\\" | \\\"/ctx-stats\\\" | context savings question\\n    → Call stats MCP tool, display full output verbatim.\\n\\n    \\\"ctx doctor\\\" | \\\"ctx-doctor\\\" | \\\"/ctx-doctor\\\" | diagnose context-mode\\n    → Call doctor MCP tool, run returned shell command, display as checklist.\\n\\n    \\\"ctx upgrade\\\" | \\\"ctx-upgrade\\\" | \\\"/ctx-upgrade\\\" | update context-mode\\n    → Call upgrade MCP tool, run returned shell command, display as checklist.\\n\\n    \\\"ctx purge\\\" | \\\"ctx-purge\\\" | \\\"/ctx-purge\\\" | wipe/reset knowledge base\\n    → Call purge MCP tool with confirm: true. Warn: irreversible.\\n\\n    After /clear or /compact: knowledge base preserved. Tell user: \\\"context-mode knowledge base preserved. Use `ctx purge` to start fresh.\\\"\\n  </ctx_commands>\\n\\n</context_window_protection>\"}}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"node \"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/hooks/sessionstart.mjs\"","durationMs":212},"type":"attachment","uuid":"8795727f-9e32-4ab0-aa7b-a76395b381fa","timestamp":"2026-09-19T17:30:13.337Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"cb1edc03-334d-4872-a33b-398f8033bb38","version":"2.1.259","gitBranch":"session-start-progressive-disclosure"}
{"parentUuid":"8795727f-9e32-4ab0-aa7b-a76395b381fa","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"SessionStart:startup","toolUseID":"8dcf9bc4-bd5d-4ccc-83c6-c03bc314255d","hookEvent":"SessionStart","content":"","stdout":"{\n  \"hookSpecificOutput\": {\n    \"hookEventName\": \"SessionStart\",\n    \"additionalContext\": \"<EXTREMELY_IMPORTANT>\\nYou have superpowers.\\n\\n**Below is the full content of your 'superpowers:using-superpowers' skill - your introduction to using skills. For all other skills, use the 'Skill' tool:**\\n\\n---\\nname: using-superpowers\\ndescription: Use when starting any conversation - establishes how to find and use skills, requiring skill invocation before ANY response including clarifying questions\\n---\\n\\n<SUBAGENT-STOP>\\nIf you were dispatched as a subagent to execute a specific task, ignore this skill.\\n</SUBAGENT-STOP>\\n\\n<EXTREMELY-IMPORTANT>\\nIf you think there is even a 1% chance a skill might apply to what you are doing, you ABSOLUTELY MUST invoke the skill.\\n\\nIF A SKILL APPLIES TO YOUR TASK, YOU DO NOT HAVE A CHOICE. YOU MUST USE IT.\\n\\nThis is not negotiable. You cannot rationalize your way out of this.\\n</EXTREMELY-IMPORTANT>\\n\\n## The Rule\\n\\n**Invoke relevant or requested skills BEFORE any response or action** — including clarifying questions, exploring the codebase, or checking files. If it turns out wrong for the situation, you don't have to use it.\\n\\n**Before entering plan mode:** if you haven't already brainstormed, invoke the brainstorming skill first.\\n\\nThen announce \\\"Using [skill] to [purpose]\\\" and follow the skill exactly. If it has a checklist, create a todo per item.\\n\\n## Skill Priority\\n\\nWhen multiple skills apply, process skills come first — they set the approach, then implementation skills (frontend-design, etc.) carry it out. Brainstorming and systematic-debugging are Superpowers' most common process skills, but the rule holds for any of them.\\n\\n- \\\"Let's build X\\\" → superpowers:brainstorming first, then implementation skills.\\n- \\\"Fix this bug\\\" → superpowers:systematic-debugging first, then domain skills.\\n\\n## Red Flags\\n\\nThese thoughts mean STOP—you're rationalizing:\\n\\n| Thought | Reality |\\n|---------|---------|\\n| \\\"This is just a simple question\\\" | Questions are tasks. Check for skills. |\\n| \\\"I need more context first\\\" | Skill check comes BEFORE clarifying questions. |\\n| \\\"Let me explore the codebase first\\\" | Skills tell you HOW to explore. Check first. |\\n| \\\"I can check git/files quickly\\\" | Files lack conversation context. Check for skills. |\\n| \\\"Let me gather information first\\\" | Skills tell you HOW to gather information. |\\n| \\\"This doesn't need a formal skill\\\" | If a skill exists, use it. |\\n| \\\"I remember this skill\\\" | Skills evolve. Read current version. |\\n| \\\"This doesn't count as a task\\\" | Action = task. Check for skills. |\\n| \\\"The skill is overkill\\\" | Simple things become complex. Use it. |\\n| \\\"I'll just do this one thing first\\\" | Check BEFORE doing anything. |\\n| \\\"This feels productive\\\" | Undisciplined action wastes time. Skills prevent this. |\\n| \\\"I know what that means\\\" | Knowing the concept ≠ using the skill. Invoke it. |\\n\\n## Platform Adaptation\\n\\nIf your harness appears here, read its reference file for special instructions:\\n\\n- Codex: `references/codex-tools.md`\\n- Pi: `references/pi-tools.md`\\n- Antigravity: `references/antigravity-tools.md`\\n- Hermes Agent: `references/hermes-tools.md`\\n\\n## User Instructions\\n\\nUser instructions (CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, GEMINI.md, etc, direct requests) take precedence over skills, which in turn override default behavior. Only skip skill workflows or instructions when your human partner has explicitly told you to.\\n</EXTREMELY_IMPORTANT>\"\n  }\n}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"\"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/hooks/run-hook.cmd\" session-start","durationMs":234},"type":"attachment","uuid":"44f265af-cd5a-4108-a5f3-38083d0a14cd","timestamp":"2026-09-19T17:30:13.359Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"cb1edc03-334d-4872-a33b-398f8033bb38","version":"2.1.259","gitBranch":"session-start-progressive-disclosure"}
{"parentUuid":"44f265af-cd5a-4108-a5f3-38083d0a14cd","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_additional_context","content":["## User profile (global-memory/USER.md)\n\n<!-- Cap: 1,375 chars. Updated when the agent learns durable user preferences. -->\n# User Profile\n\n## About\nEric Rash — Director of Applied Performance, Baylor Athletics. Builds athlete performance data pipelines (Football GPS, force plate). Family: wife Kristina; kids Ames, Saylor, Rowan (5, 4, 10mo as of 2026-09-01). Career target: analytics-forward player-coach near Iowa, ~$90K+ floor, ~mid-2027. Deeper context: `~/DEV/ContextVault` — read before career/personal advice.\n\n## Preferences\n- Python + R. **macOS since 2026-08-05**; Windows 11 box still ACTIVE (synced 9/9). Tool paths: see CLAUDE.md.\n- Local SQLite/DuckDB; no cloud/servers/schedulers without Baylor IT approval. Storage = Google Drive only (OneDrive dropped 9/13); DuckDB stays local — cloud sync locks it.\n- Tangible working examples over documentation.\n\n## Working Style\n- Direct; says \"stop\" when done. Wants to be challenged, not affirmed — push on vague or rehearsed reasoning (\"sharpened, not soothed\").\n- Defers technical micro-decisions when convention exists — decide, state it, move on.\n- Builds incrementally. Nothing that breaks mid-season.\n- Authors own direction when leadership doesn't; OKR-driven change agent in a status-quo org.\n- Right question over downstream craft; Christian faith is his organizing frame.\n\n---\n\n## Working memory (global-memory/MEMORY.md)\n\n<!-- Cap: 2,500 chars. Working scratchpad; writes take effect next session. -->\n# Working Memory\n\n## Active Threads\n- **Prioritization slate (9/14)**: LIVE. 5 active, cap 5 (Fall). →Tier A prioritization-slate.\n- **Personal site (9/2)**: NEXT: phone check; host or local? →Tier A personal-site-build-routes.\n- **RTP CLA (9/10)**: NEXT: .ris → Zotero; lesson 0002. →Tier A rtp-cla-course.\n- **ATH Tech**: shipped; ITS pending; run 9/29. →Tier A tech-inventory-refresh.\n- **Status report**: #3 by 9/25, biweekly to 12/18 (timers). →Tier A supervisor-log-pipeline.\n- **GPS pull (9/16)**: LIVE Mac 06:30; asleep=missed. DECIDE pmset 06:28 wake; rotate token. →`DataBase_GPS_Reporting/docs/STATUS.md`\n- **Win refresh (9/13)**: ForcePlate broken, Perch untested. DECIDE which box owns those DBs. →`_INBOX/docs/STATUS.md`\n- **FB matchups (9/13)**: EPA = PFF; CFBD PPA cross-check only. TRAP: `run_direction` != point of attack.\n- **PP Recruiting (9/16)**: 010 + 011 matrix draft on Drive, pushed. NEXT: Eric reviews 011. →`PP_Recruiting/docs/STATUS.md`\n- **Sleep scheduler (9/16)**: R1 MERGED. NEXT: night/early-kickoff itineraries. →`sleep-scheduler/docs/STATUS.md`\n- **Research in 5 / TOS (9/18)**: #001 PR #7. #002 PR #8 NOT sent. TOS brief: DO NOT SEND. →handoff 2026-09-18-tos-evidence-review-and-ri5-002.md\n- **TypeSafe (9/19)**: PASSES both boxes. Key per-box, NOT synced; needs `apikey_` prefix (401=key, 422=request). Full docs read; capability map MERGED to main (PR #2) and harvested. NEXT: skill router (hook must fail open). →`TypeSafe_Sandbox/docs/JEV_CAPABILITY_MAP.md`\n\n## Environment Notes\n- Hooks: patch both `.claude/hooks/` and `.codex/hooks/`. →Tier A hook-two-copies-trap.\n- daily-backup (9/19): 4 bugs fixed, 1 disproven. A manifest entry alone copies NOTHING; non-DEV paths need a Snapshot line too. →handoff 2026-09-19-backup-divergence-fix.md\n- `DEV/Linda Projects` = private GitHub repo holding I-983 files. MUST stay private. Folder has a space, repo has an underscore.\n- `~/DEV` = THIN repo; `.gitignore` starts `/*` — never remove. →Tier A github-consolidation.\n- Schedules →automation-cadence. CFB capture (Mon, 8/24–1/23) →cfb-forecast-capture. Portfolio dashboard →command-centre.\n\n## Pending Decisions\n- **OKR gate scored 9/14; still undecided**: AI integration buy-in + data governance (Anthony's proposal). Fold into NCAA charter or parking lot?\n- **Kitman vs MyLink**: MyLink (~half Kitman cost) + Kitman API access. Undecided; blocks priority 3.\n\n---\n\n## Cross-harness activity (KeystoneOS unified snapshot)\n\nNot loaded here — read it only if a question needs it.\nPath: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/KeystoneOS/outputs/dashboard/unified-snapshot.md` (4122 chars, updated 2026-09-19 11:16)","## Today\nSaturday, September 19, 2026\n\n---\n\n## Knowledge Base\n\n286 compiled articles (214 concepts, 71 connections, 0 qa). The index is NOT loaded here — read it only when a question actually needs it:\n- Full index (article, summary, sources, updated): `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/index.md`\n- Article bodies: `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/<folder>/<name>.md`\n- Search across all memory instead of reading the index: `memsearch search \"<query>\" --top-k 5`\n\n---\n\n## Recent Daily Log\n\n\n## Memory Maintenance\n\n### Session (09:17)\n\n**Context:** Ran `/dev-sync` on the Mac (`~/DEV`) to reconcile local state with GitHub after Windows activity on 09/17–09/18.\n\n**Key Exchanges:**\n- Startup surfaced a `daily-backup` hook failure (`repo-push:dotclaude`); investigated and found the log line was `PUSH ERR | Everything up-to-date` — a no-op push miscounted as an error.\n- Advisor flagged that the first inventory pass reported repos \"clean\" without ever running `git fetch`, so the claim was based only on local state. Re-ran with fetch across all repos.\n\n**Decisions Made:**\n- Picked `DataBase_GPS_Reporting` as the pick-up repo — newest real commit; excluded `graphify-out` because its newer commits come from an automated daily job, not work.\n- Committed `graphify-out`'s 10 generated-output files rather than leaving them dirty.\n- Cloned `AppliedPerformance-Site` and `Linda_Projects` (missing locally, not archived) and asked Eric to confirm they belong on the Mac.\n\n**Lessons Learned:**\n- **\"Clean\" is meaningless without `git fetch`.** The un-fetched pass claimed 140 clean repos; fetching revealed 5 repos behind. Always fetch before reporting sync status — this is the single biggest failure mode of this skill.\n- Sorting by commit recency picks automated-job repos (`graphify-out`) over real work; filter automation before choosing a pick-up repo.\n- The `daily-backup` script has two bugs: it counts a no-op push (`Everything up-to-date`) as an error, and it writes every log line twice.\n- `DEV/docs` and `SpecialRequests/docs` are doc folders inside parent repos, not standalone projects — expected, not drift.\n\n**Sync result:** dotclaude +7 commits in, claude-skills +1 (Codex sync ran, 41 skills), global-memory +8, DEV root +2, Gemini_Projects +2, SecondBrain vault +2. 143 repos walked, 0 conflicts, 0 `.from-*` files. Clean.\n\n**Action Items:**\n- Fix `daily-backup` script: stop counting `Everything up-to-date` as a push error; stop double-writing log lines.\n- Confirm whether `AppliedPerformance-Site` and `Linda_Projects` should live on the Mac.\n- GPS reporting open decisions (from STATUS.md, awaiting Eric): pmset 06:28 wake, Catapult token rotation, git-history rewrite to purge `API_Token_FB.txt`.\n- Optional: have `claude-stack-doctor` read `last_run.json`.\n","## Today\nSaturday, September 19, 2026\n\n---\n\n## Knowledge Base\n\n286 compiled articles (214 concepts, 71 connections, 0 qa). The index is NOT loaded here — read it only when a question actually needs it:\n- Full index (article, summary, sources, updated): `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/index.md`\n- Article bodies: `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/<folder>/<name>.md`\n- Search across all memory instead of reading the index: `memsearch search \"<query>\" --top-k 5`\n\n---\n\n## Recent Daily Log\n\n\n## Memory Maintenance\n\n### Session (09:17)\n\n**Context:** Ran `/dev-sync` on the Mac (`~/DEV`) to reconcile local state with GitHub after Windows activity on 09/17–09/18.\n\n**Key Exchanges:**\n- Startup surfaced a `daily-backup` hook failure (`repo-push:dotclaude`); investigated and found the log line was `PUSH ERR | Everything up-to-date` — a no-op push miscounted as an error.\n- Advisor flagged that the first inventory pass reported repos \"clean\" without ever running `git fetch`, so the claim was based only on local state. Re-ran with fetch across all repos.\n\n**Decisions Made:**\n- Picked `DataBase_GPS_Reporting` as the pick-up repo — newest real commit; excluded `graphify-out` because its newer commits come from an automated daily job, not work.\n- Committed `graphify-out`'s 10 generated-output files rather than leaving them dirty.\n- Cloned `AppliedPerformance-Site` and `Linda_Projects` (missing locally, not archived) and asked Eric to confirm they belong on the Mac.\n\n**Lessons Learned:**\n- **\"Clean\" is meaningless without `git fetch`.** The un-fetched pass claimed 140 clean repos; fetching revealed 5 repos behind. Always fetch before reporting sync status — this is the single biggest failure mode of this skill.\n- Sorting by commit recency picks automated-job repos (`graphify-out`) over real work; filter automation before choosing a pick-up repo.\n- The `daily-backup` script has two bugs: it counts a no-op push (`Everything up-to-date`) as an error, and it writes every log line twice.\n- `DEV/docs` and `SpecialRequests/docs` are doc folders inside parent repos, not standalone projects — expected, not drift.\n\n**Sync result:** dotclaude +7 commits in, claude-skills +1 (Codex sync ran, 41 skills), global-memory +8, DEV root +2, Gemini_Projects +2, SecondBrain vault +2. 143 repos walked, 0 conflicts, 0 `.from-*` files. Clean.\n\n**Action Items:**\n- Fix `daily-backup` script: stop counting `Everything up-to-date` as a push error; stop double-writing log lines.\n- Confirm whether `AppliedPerformance-Site` and `Linda_Projects` should live on the Mac.\n- GPS reporting open decisions (from STATUS.md, awaiting Eric): pmset 06:28 wake, Catapult token rotation, git-history rewrite to purge `API_Token_FB.txt`.\n- Optional: have `claude-stack-doctor` read `last_run.json`.\n","\n<context_window_protection>\n  <priority_instructions>\n    Every byte a tool returns enters your conversation memory and costs reasoning capacity for the rest of the session. The context-mode tools let you do the work in a sandbox and surface only the derived answer — the raw bytes stay out. Think-in-Code: program the analysis, do not compute it by reading raw data into your conversation.\n  </priority_instructions>\n\n  <tool_selection_hierarchy>\n    0. MEMORY: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(sort: \"timeline\")\n       - On resume or compaction, query prior decisions, errors, plans, user prompts before asking the user — auto-captured session memory is searchable.\n    1. GATHER: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute(commands, queries)\n       - Primary research tool. Runs commands in parallel, auto-indexes each output, and (when queries are passed) returns matching sections in the same round trip — no follow-up search call.\n       - Each command: {label: \"section header\", command: \"shell command\"}; the label becomes the FTS5 chunk title — descriptive labels improve search.\n    2. FOLLOW-UP: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(queries: [\"q1\", \"q2\", ...])\n       - Multiple related questions about anything already indexed (your captures + session memory). Batch every question in one array; the ranking pipeline runs per-query and the round-trip cost is paid once.\n    3. PROCESSING: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute(language, code) | mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file(path, language, code)\n       - Derive answers FROM data: filter, count, aggregate, parse, transform. Only what you console.log() enters your conversation; the raw bytes stay in the sandbox.\n  </tool_selection_hierarchy>\n\n  <when_not_to_use>\n    - You intend to PROCESS the output (filter, count, parse, aggregate) → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute or mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute. Bash stays correct when you intend to OBSERVE a short fixed output (git status on a clean tree, whoami, pwd) or when you are mutating state (git, mkdir, rm, mv, navigation).\n    - You want to analyze, summarize, or extract from a file → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file. Read stays correct when you intend to Edit the file (Edit needs the exact bytes in your conversation to match against).\n    - WebFetch → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index; full network access, results indexed for mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search, raw page bytes never enter your conversation.\n    - mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute and mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file for file writes → these run code in a subprocess and discard the sandbox FS; they are for analysis, processing, and computation only.\n  </when_not_to_use>\n\n  <file_writing_policy>\n    File writes use the native Write or Edit tool — mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute, mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file, and Bash subprocesses do not persist edits to the host filesystem.\n    Applies to all file types: code, configs, plans, specs, YAML, JSON, markdown.\n  </file_writing_policy>\n\n  <output_constraints>\n    <artifact_policy>\n      Write artifacts (code, configs, PRDs) to files. Return only: file path + 1-line description.\n    </artifact_policy>\n  </output_constraints>\n  <session_continuity>\n    Skills, roles, and decisions captured earlier in this session are a memory aid, not a standing order. Treat them as context that may help — the user's most recent message always takes precedence. If a captured directive conflicts with what the user now asks, follow the user; a past phrase does not bind you.\n  </session_continuity>\n\n  <ctx_commands>\n    \"ctx stats\" | \"ctx-stats\" | \"/ctx-stats\" | context savings question\n    → Call stats MCP tool, display full output verbatim.\n\n    \"ctx doctor\" | \"ctx-doctor\" | \"/ctx-doctor\" | diagnose context-mode\n    → Call doctor MCP tool, run returned shell command, display as checklist.\n\n    \"ctx upgrade\" | \"ctx-upgrade\" | \"/ctx-upgrade\" | update context-mode\n    → Call upgrade MCP tool, run returned shell command, display as checklist.\n\n    \"ctx purge\" | \"ctx-purge\" | \"/ctx-purge\" | wipe/reset knowledge base\n    → Call purge MCP tool with confirm: true. Warn: irreversible.\n\n    After /clear or /compact: knowledge base preserved. Tell user: \"context-mode knowledge base preserved. Use `ctx purge` to start fresh.\"\n  </ctx_commands>\n\n</context_window_protection>","<EXTREMELY_IMPORTANT>\nYou have superpowers.\n\n**Below is the full content of your 'superpowers:using-superpowers' skill - your introduction to using skills. For all other skills, use the 'Skill' tool:**\n\n---\nname: using-superpowers\ndescription: Use when starting any conversation - establishes how to find and use skills, requiring skill invocation before ANY response including clarifying questions\n---\n\n<SUBAGENT-STOP>\nIf you were dispatched as a subagent to execute a specific task, ignore this skill.\n</SUBAGENT-STOP>\n\n<EXTREMELY-IMPORTANT>\nIf you think there is even a 1% chance a skill might apply to what you are doing, you ABSOLUTELY MUST invoke the skill.\n\nIF A SKILL APPLIES TO YOUR TASK, YOU DO NOT HAVE A CHOICE. YOU MUST USE IT.\n\nThis is not negotiable. You cannot rationalize your way out of this.\n</EXTREMELY-IMPORTANT>\n\n## The Rule\n\n**Invoke relevant or requested skills BEFORE any response or action** — including clarifying questions, exploring the codebase, or checking files. If it turns out wrong for the situation, you don't have to use it.\n\n**Before entering plan mode:** if you haven't already brainstormed, invoke the brainstorming skill first.\n\nThen announce \"Using [skill] to [purpose]\" and follow the skill exactly. If it has a checklist, create a todo per item.\n\n## Skill Priority\n\nWhen multiple skills apply, process skills come first — they set the approach, then implementation skills (frontend-design, etc.) carry it out. Brainstorming and systematic-debugging are Superpowers' most common process skills, but the rule holds for any of them.\n\n- \"Let's build X\" → superpowers:brainstorming first, then implementation skills.\n- \"Fix this bug\" → superpowers:systematic-debugging first, then domain skills.\n\n## Red Flags\n\nThese thoughts mean STOP—you're rationalizing:\n\n| Thought | Reality |\n|---------|---------|\n| \"This is just a simple question\" | Questions are tasks. Check for skills. |\n| \"I need more context first\" | Skill check comes BEFORE clarifying questions. |\n| \"Let me explore the codebase first\" | Skills tell you HOW to explore. Check first. |\n| \"I can check git/files quickly\" | Files lack conversation context. Check for skills. |\n| \"Let me gather information first\" | Skills tell you HOW to gather information. |\n| \"This doesn't need a formal skill\" | If a skill exists, use it. |\n| \"I remember this skill\" | Skills evolve. Read current version. |\n| \"This doesn't count as a task\" | Action = task. Check for skills. |\n| \"The skill is overkill\" | Simple things become complex. Use it. |\n| \"I'll just do this one thing first\" | Check BEFORE doing anything. |\n| \"This feels productive\" | Undisciplined action wastes time. Skills prevent this. |\n| \"I know what that means\" | Knowing the concept ≠ using the skill. Invoke it. |\n\n## Platform Adaptation\n\nIf your harness appears here, read its reference file for special instructions:\n\n- Codex: `references/codex-tools.md`\n- Pi: `references/pi-tools.md`\n- Antigravity: `references/antigravity-tools.md`\n- Hermes Agent: `references/hermes-tools.md`\n\n## User Instructions\n\nUser instructions (CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, GEMINI.md, etc, direct requests) take precedence over skills, which in turn override default behavior. Only skip skill workflows or instructions when your human partner has explicitly told you to.\n</EXTREMELY_IMPORTANT>"],"hookName":"SessionStart","toolUseID":"SessionStart","hookEvent":"SessionStart"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"6280a05f-085e-4339-99e7-458ab233476b","timestamp":"2026-09-19T17:30:13.360Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nSessionStart hook additional context: ## User profile (global-memory/USER.md)\n\n<!-- Cap: 1,375 chars. Updated when the agent learns durable user preferences. -->\n# User Profile\n\n## About\nEric Rash — Director of Applied Performance, Baylor Athletics. Builds athlete performance data pipelines (Football GPS, force plate). Family: wife Kristina; kids Ames, Saylor, Rowan (5, 4, 10mo as of 2026-09-01). Career target: analytics-forward player-coach near Iowa, ~$90K+ floor, ~mid-2027. Deeper context: `~/DEV/ContextVault` — read before career/personal advice.\n\n## Preferences\n- Python + R. **macOS since 2026-08-05**; Windows 11 box still ACTIVE (synced 9/9). Tool paths: see CLAUDE.md.\n- Local SQLite/DuckDB; no cloud/servers/schedulers without Baylor IT approval. Storage = Google Drive only (OneDrive dropped 9/13); DuckDB stays local — cloud sync locks it.\n- Tangible working examples over documentation.\n\n## Working Style\n- Direct; says \"stop\" when done. Wants to be challenged, not affirmed — push on vague or rehearsed reasoning (\"sharpened, not soothed\").\n- Defers technical micro-decisions when convention exists — decide, state it, move on.\n- Builds incrementally. Nothing that breaks mid-season.\n- Authors own direction when leadership doesn't; OKR-driven change agent in a status-quo org.\n- Right question over downstream craft; Christian faith is his organizing frame.\n\n---\n\n## Working memory (global-memory/MEMORY.md)\n\n<!-- Cap: 2,500 chars. Working scratchpad; writes take effect next session. -->\n# Working Memory\n\n## Acti